{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST supply the review prompt that each group's reviewer follows. The prompt MUST direct the reviewer not to edit files. It MUST direct the reviewer to make mechanical changes itself in the revised text it returns, and to return design questions for the user.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has three prompt obligations. The skill supplies the review prompt, and that prompt directs the reviewer (a) not to edit files, (b) to make mechanical changes itself in the revised text it returns, and (c) to return design questions for the user.\n\nThe single test, `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_review_prompt_makes_mechanical_changes_and_returns_design_questions]`, reads the actual shipped prompt. It does this through the helper `review_prompt()`, which loads `references/review-prompt.md` from the `habeas-requirements` skill. It collapses the whitespace with `collapse` and uses `assert_text`, which panics if a phrase is missing. It asserts three phrases:\n- \"Don't edit files; make your changes in the revised text you return\" covers the no-editing directive and the instruction to put changes in the returned revised text.\n- \"**Mechanical changes:** make them yourself in the revised text, and never phrase them as questions\" covers the mechanical-changes obligation.\n- \"**Design questions:** return these; the user decides\" covers returning design questions for the user.\n\nI confirmed that each phrase appears in `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/references/review-prompt.md` as a directive. The \"Don't edit files\" phrase is in the opening instructions, and the other two are in the \"Two kinds of finding\" section. The prompt is therefore not merely mentioning the phrases in passing.\n\nThe prompt is a text asset, so a phrase-presence check is the natural way to test it. The test runs against the real file, so removing or weakening any of the three directives would fail it. The part of the obligation about the skill supplying the prompt to reviewers belongs to `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.review.inputs`, which has its own test. Other prompt details (report template, names, criteria and so on) belong to the sibling `review_prompt.*` requirements, which have their own tests. I found no unacknowledged gap.",
  "references": "a1764fd961e7ff309b1190a1565f82dcbb9bd15698c40181c2ef1887c4f3d160",
